# The Highland bagpipe's three drones in one voice, played at A2 so they
# take one converter: the bass drone (A2) and the two tenors (A3), an
# octave above it, so the tenors' partials fall on A2's even harmonics.
# Each drone is a cylindrical bore on a single cane reed: the odd
# harmonics lead, rich far up. A drone's harmonic k at (1 if k is odd,
# 0.4 if even) k^-0.7 / (1 + (k f0 / 2500)^2); the voice's harmonic k
# the bass drone's (f0 110 Hz) plus, for an even k, sqrt(2) times a
# tenor's k / 2 (f0 220 Hz; two tenors at equal power), scaled to the
# loudest; the amplitudes at full precision. They swell as the bag
# fills and die away when it empties. No céleste: its second read at
# half the rate put a steady cluster of images about 10 kHz (8-12 kHz 10
# dB louder with it), heard as a constant high pitch.
